  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nCurrent satellite and 500mb analysis reveals the upper ridge \nremains in control across the southeastern US with weak \nsouthwesterly flow aloft across southern California. The further \ndisplacement of the upper ridge allows for the continuation of the\ncooling trend observed through the last few days. High \ntemperatures this afternoon are expected to be about 3-5 degrees \ncooler compared to yesterday. This has high temperatures running \naround average to 5 degrees below climatological averages - a much\nwelcome change from the heat seen last week. Dewpoints and \nhumidity remain high for the coastal areas, keeping overnight \ntemperatures on the mild side while portions of the inland valleys\nwill be allowed to fall into upper 50s. \n\nMoving into Tuesday, an embedded shortwave trough within a much \nlarger upper trough across the northeast Pacific is progged to eject \ninto central California. This will bring around 40 kts of southwest \nflow at 500mb for Tuesday-Wednesday, leading to breezy conditions in \nthe mountains and adjacent deserts each afternoon. West to southwest \nwinds generally 25-35 mph for the mountains and deserts, locally up \nto 45-50 mph within major mountain passes. While this is largely sub-\nadvisory or headline levels, the main concern will be elevated to \nnear-critical fire weather conditions in the mountains and deserts \nwhere afternoon humidity is expected to fall into the 20-25% range \nfor the mountains and near 15% in the deserts. Temperatures will \nremain on the cool side through midweek, with highs a degree or two \ncooler than today.\n\nThursday into early next week...\n\nFollowing the shortwave passing through Tuesday/Wednesday, \nsouthwesterly flow aloft stays in place as the large-scale trough \nremains in place across the western US. This will likely keep the \nbelow normal temperatures and breezy onshore wind in place through \nthe end of the week. However, the evolution of this large upper \ntrough and embedded shortwaves will play a pivotal role in the \nsteering flow of a potential tropical cyclone. This potential storm \nis currently only Invest 95E as a cluster of disorganized \nthunderstorms off the far southern Mexican coast. There remains a \nsignificant amount of uncertainty in the expected evolution of \nthis system, especially as a storm has yet to officially develop. \nThe current spread of the grand ensemble favors 2 main clusters, \none with a more westward track out to sea, and the other as a more\nnorthward track which could bring any remnants towards southern \nCalifornia. Latest ensembles are favoring more of the westward \ntrack, with only a ~5% chance of more direct/significant impacts \nto the region. Despite this uncertainty and the potential track of\nthis storm, chances of precipitation increase as early as \nSaturday evening with the potential for an influx of tropical \nmoisture aloft. Even still, plenty of cards need to fall in the \nright way for this to occur, so stay tuned over the next few days\nas the forecast becomes more refined and details become more \nclear.",
